<%# A row's actions behind one trigger.

    **Why.** Three labelled ghosts per row made the trailing cell 253-280px - the widest column in every
    admin table, 36% of the visible width at 1024px, and 455px at 1920 where it absorbs all the slack to
    hold two buttons. That cell was the overflow. Polaris's IndexTable, GitHub, Linear and Stripe all put
    row actions behind one trigger for the same reason.

    **`popover`, not an absolute panel.** Measured: a panel in this cell reaches 508px while its scroll
    wrapper ends at 288px, and `.table-wrapper` computes `overflow: auto` on both axes - so an ordinary
    dropdown is clipped by the table. `popover` renders in the top layer, which nothing clips. It also
    degrades honestly: `popovertarget` opens the panel with no JavaScript at all, just unanchored, so the
    actions stay reachable if the controller never connects.

    **The trigger carries the row's name.** `lucide_icon` is `aria-hidden`, so an icon-only control has no
    accessible name at all - and "Actions" alone would be twenty identical controls on one page. This is
    the same reason these actions were given visible labels in the first place; the name moves into the
    trigger rather than being lost.

    Locals:
      label - names the row: "Actions for Grade 6 Homeroom"
      id    - unique, since a popover is addressed by id %>
<div class="inline-flex" data-controller="row-actions">
  <button type="button"
          popovertarget="<%= id %>"
          class="tw-menu-trigger"
          data-row-actions-target="trigger"
          data-testid="row-actions-trigger">
    <span class="sr-only"><%= label %></span>
    <%= lucide_icon "ellipsis-vertical", class: "size-5 shrink-0", aria: { hidden: true } %>
  </button>

  <div id="<%= id %>"
       popover
       class="tw-menu-panel"
       data-row-actions-target="panel"
       data-action="toggle->row-actions#toggled"
       data-testid="row-actions-panel">
    <%= yield %>
  </div>
</div>
